1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,617
Nearly a thousand PhDs got this puzzle wrong. Your turn.

2
00:00:04,617 --> 00:00:07,765
One of three briefcases has a million bucks. So you pick one.

3
00:00:08,015 --> 00:00:11,214
The host knows where the money is, and always opens an empty one.

4
00:00:11,464 --> 00:00:13,921
Then he offers a swap, and it's fifty-fifty, right?

5
00:00:14,171 --> 00:00:15,955
Hit like if you said fifty-fifty.

6
00:00:16,255 --> 00:00:20,246
Nope. Your first pick was a one in three guess, and opening a case changed nothing.

7
00:00:20,496 --> 00:00:22,345
So switching wins two out of three.

8
00:00:23,645 --> 00:00:26,507
Send this to the friend who's— who's already typing 'no way'.

9
00:00:26,807 --> 00:00:32,997
A columnist printed that in nineteen ninety, and by her count, nearly a thousand PhDs wrote in saying she's wrong.

10
00:00:33,597 --> 00:00:36,323
Still don't buy it? Follow, this page ends the argument.

11
00:00:37,037 --> 00:00:40,808
Make it a hundred cases: you pick one, and he opens ninety-eight empties.

12
00:00:41,908 --> 00:00:44,825
Now switching wins ninety-nine percent. Same game.

13
00:00:46,125 --> 00:00:48,041
So… stay or switch? Your turn.
